      <div class="kn-list">
        <article class="kn-item">
          <span class="num">01</span>
          <div>
            <span class="badge badge-live" style="margin-bottom:8px;">Published</span>
            <h3>How to choose the right abrasive media for your application</h3>
            <p>A step-by-step decision path from material, profile and equipment to a concrete media shortlist.</p>
            <details>
              <summary>Read full article</summary>
              <div class="kn-article">
                <p>Choosing the right abrasive media comes down to six questions, answered in order:</p>
                <h4>1. What is your base material?</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Ferrous (carbon steel, cast iron)</strong> — the widest media family is available: steel shot (A/B), steel grit (C/D), grinding shot (F/G/H/N) and recycled cut wire (E).</li>
                  <li><strong>Stainless steel</strong> — you must use stainless shot (J/K/L/M) or stainless cut wire (O/P) to avoid carbon contamination that causes rust spots.</li>
                  <li><strong>Non-ferrous (aluminum, copper, zinc)</strong> — copper cut wire (I) or stainless media; never carbon steel media (causes iron smut and galvanic corrosion).</li>
                </ul>
                <h4>2. What surface treatment do you need?</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Cleaning / rust removal</strong> — spherical media (steel shot A, or stainless shot J for stainless bases).</li>
                  <li><strong>Descaling / roughening</strong> — angular media (steel grit C, or bearing grit D for longer life).</li>
                  <li><strong>Shot peening</strong> — spherical media only, to induce residual compressive stress; steel shot A for general parts, grinding shot G82B (H-grade) for heavy-duty peening.</li>
                  <li><strong>Precision cleaning / polishing</strong> — fine spherical media, low power.</li>
                </ul>
                <h4>3. Target roughness Rz?</h4>
                <p>Rz determines particle size. For steel shot: S170 (&lt;50 μm), S230 (50-80), S280 (50-100), S330/S390 (80-120+). For steel grit: G80 → G16 as roughness increases. Stainless and cut wire use diameter φ (0.4 mm → 1.4 mm).</p>
                <h4>4. Equipment type?</h4>
                <p>Wheel blasting (抛丸机) handles both shot and grit. Pressure blasting (喷砂机) with shot peening must use grit only — never shot or grinding shot (they jam and break the nozzle).</p>
                <h4>5. Hardness grade?</h4>
                <p>For steel shot/grit: GP (42-52 HRC, general), GL (52-61 HRC, shot peening), GH (&gt;61 HRC, special high-wear). Stainless, copper, cut wire and grinding shot do not use this scale.</p>
                <h4>6. Annual throughput?</h4>
                <p>Multiply throughput (tons) × industry consumption rate (kg/ton) ÷ 1000 × recovery factor. With a recovery system the factor is 1; without it, 1.6 (you consume 60% more). ≥300 t/yr qualifies for on-site visit and custom blending.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:12px;"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Use the selector above to get a ranked recommendation in seconds, then request the PDF report for the full cost model.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="kn-item">
          <span class="num">02</span>
          <div>
            <span class="badge badge-live" style="margin-bottom:8px;">Published</span>
            <h3>Blast media price vs. total blasting cost</h3>
            <p>Why cheap media is rarely the cheapest option &mdash; and how to calculate the difference honestly.</p>
            <details>
              <summary>Read full article</summary>
              <div class="kn-article">
                <p>Most buyers compare media on <strong>price per kg</strong>. That is the single biggest mistake in abrasive procurement. Here is why:</p>
                <h4>The real formula</h4>
                <p style="background:var(--bg-soft); padding:10px 14px; border-radius:8px; font-size:13px;"><strong>Cost per ton of workpiece</strong> = (media unit price × tons consumed + power + labor + equipment depreciation &amp; maintenance + dust control − scrap recovery) ÷ workpiece tons</p>
                <h4>Why cheaper media costs more</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Higher consumption</strong> — low-quality steel shot shatters after 500-800 cycles vs. 1500-2000 for GP-grade. You buy 2-3× more kg to blast the same ton of steel.</li>
                  <li><strong>More downtime</strong> — broken media clogs the recovery system, wears blades and causes more frequent media changes.</li>
                  <li><strong>Higher dust cost</strong> — shattered fines increase dust load, raising filter replacement and disposal cost.</li>
                  <li><strong>Lower scrap value</strong> — good steel shot retains 40-60% of its value as recyclable scrap; broken fines are near-worthless.</li>
                </ul>
                <h4>Worked example</h4>
                <p>Steel structure plant, 5,000 t/yr throughput, wheel blasting, with recovery:</p>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Scenario A (cheap shot, $0.60/kg):</strong> 0.6 kg/t × 5000 t = 3 t consumed → media cost $1,800; higher power + dust + downtime → total cost per ton ≈ $2.10.</li>
                  <li><strong>Scenario B (GP-grade shot, $0.90/kg):</strong> 0.4 kg/t × 5000 t = 2 t consumed → media cost $1,800; lower power + dust + downtime → total cost per ton ≈ $1.55.</li>
                  <li><strong>Result:</strong> Scenario B saves $0.55/ton = $2,750/yr, even though the media costs 50% more per kg.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin-top:12px;"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Use the A/B Cost Comparison tool above with your real numbers. The formula works for any currency and any media pair — and it is the single most effective technical-marketing argument you can make to a customer.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="kn-item">
          <span class="num">03</span>
          <div>
            <span class="badge badge-live" style="margin-bottom:8px;">Published</span>
            <h3>Sa 2&frac12; vs. Sa 3: when does "cleaner" stop paying off?</h3>
            <p>Matching surface cleanliness to the coating system &mdash; and where over-specification quietly burns budget.</p>
            <details>
              <summary>Read full article</summary>
              <div class="kn-article">
                <p>ISO 8501-1 defines four blast-cleaning grades. Specifying a higher grade than your coating requires is one of the most common — and most expensive — mistakes in surface preparation.</p>
                <h4>When each grade is right</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Sa 1 (light):</strong> Maintenance blasting, removing loose paint and rust before recoating. Rarely specified for new work.</li>
                  <li><strong>Sa 2 (thorough):</strong> General maintenance, non-critical structural steel, shop primer application.</li>
                  <li><strong>Sa 2½ (very thorough):</strong> The industry default for most coating systems — epoxy primers, zinc-rich primers, polyurethane topcoats. 90% of new structural steel spec is Sa 2½.</li>
                  <li><strong>Sa 3 (visually clean steel):</strong> Required only for thermal spraying (metallizing), high-performance tank linings, and some nuclear/aerospace applications. Demands clean, dry abrasive and a controlled environment.</li>
                </ul>
                <h4>Why over-specifying burns budget</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Media consumption doubles</strong> going from Sa 2½ to Sa 3 — you blast the same surface twice to remove the last "shadows."</li>
                  <li><strong>Roughness climbs</strong> — Sa 3 on a profile-sensitive coating (e.g. thin-film epoxy) can push Rz above the coating's tolerance, causing pinholes.</li>
                  <li><strong>Reblast risk</strong> — if the surface flash-rusts before coating (especially outdoors), you reblast to Sa 3 again, doubling cost.</li>
                </ul>
                <h4>The decision rule</h4>
                <p>Always spec the <strong>lowest grade your coating supplier will accept</strong>. If the data sheet says "Sa 2½, Rz 50-80 μm," do not upgrade to Sa 3 "to be safe" — it adds 40-60% to your blasting cost with zero coating benefit. Reserve Sa 3 for thermal spray and critical tank linings where the spec demands it.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:12px;"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Check the coating data sheet first, spec the grade it requires, and confirm with your abrasive supplier that your media + equipment can hit that grade + profile consistently.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </article>

